			<section id="site-functions">
				<h2><a href="#site-functions">Site functions</a></h2>
				<article id="store">
					<h3><a href="#store">Store</a></h3>
					<p>What does it mean to Store? To freeze the state of a set of numbers or data? Will that data ultimately represent that which was meant to be stored? Is some jes-no-se-quas lost when we strike that brilliant green button as we STORE our lives away into some gargantuan matrix of data like the internet and all the devices that makes it run and connects to it?
When I was writing these pages, or rather the bulk of them, I lived alone on a hill. All manner of people would come and ask me; "why are you so alone? Why don't you come watch football and drink beer with us?" Among these pages I will attempt to answer such questions.</p>
				</article>
				<article id="access">
					<h3><a href="#access">Access</a></h3>
					<p>Accessing data is a task one must think about before attempting. One must spin up in their mind as complete a representation of the world as they can build before accessing important data. Where am I accessing? Can someone see over my shoulder? Will Big Brother be peering into the data packets and this extract my prescious secrets? Thankfully we offer multiple options so that users can rest easy. If at home with their own cozy command line, one might opt for CMD Line. Therein triffulayy one might become embraced with the blanket of secure warmth ever provided by that suckling computer which they call their own.
<br><br>
Alternatively, a user might be in the cold throes of poor security. If one finds themself in such a bleak and utterly dark place such as the public existence one will surely be in agony of all the eyes and all the wants, fears, yearnings of their fellow man. Most of which might claim to be good people and surely most are. But in such staggeringly antagonistic journeys into secure data that one might attempt, throwing off the regard of 'good ops' security and find themselves in a situation where the must indeed access that so now less secure data then one might be inclined to use the Browser option. Pay close mind to how Browser is close to Bowser -- that Lorde of Trifles indeed will surely attempt to steal and corrupt your data!
<br><br>
But be at ease O fateful user. For OpenSSL is implemented in the very browser (the good one) you useth presently and shall have such crisp solutions to your data problems on this day.</p>
				</article>
